# Project: pfSense + Wazuh SOC Home Lab — Infrastructure Setup Only

Status: Ready to start
Started:
Cost: $0
Host Machine: Kali Linux (dual-booted, bare metal)


## Scope of This Phase

This phase covers **only building the infrastructure**. You will:

- Install VirtualBox
- Create the virtual network
- Deploy pfSense
- Deploy Wazuh
- Deploy the target VM
- Get all machines communicating

You will not do any of the following yet. Those come in a later phase:

- Writing firewall rules
- Configuring Wazuh detection rules
- Connecting pfSense logs to Wazuh
- Installing Wazuh agents
- Running attacks

The goal right now is: the lab is alive and all machines can reach each other.

## Part 1 — Architecture Overview

Your Kali runs on bare metal. VirtualBox runs inside Kali. All lab machines run inside VirtualBox.

Two networks inside VirtualBox:

- NAT — used by pfSense for internet access.
- labnet — an isolated internal network where the lab machines live.

Machines:

- pfSense — firewall and gateway.
- Wazuh — SIEM server.
- Target — Ubuntu Server that will later be monitored.
- Kali — your host and attacker.

IP layout on labnet:

- pfSense LAN: 192.168.1.1
- Wazuh VM: 192.168.1.10
- Target VM: 192.168.1.20
- DHCP range (for any other machines): 192.168.1.100 to 192.168.1.200

Kali reaches the lab through a host-only adapter that you will later add to pfSense as an OPT interface.

