## Part 3 — Build Order

Follow these steps in order. Do not skip. Each step assumes the previous ones are done.

---

### Step 1 — Install VirtualBox on Kali

Open a terminal on Kali.

Run:

```
sudo apt update
sudo apt install -y virtualbox virtualbox-ext-pack
sudo apt install -y linux-headers-$(uname -r)
sudo /sbin/vboxconfig
```

Verify:

```
virtualbox --help
```

You should see usage output. Open VirtualBox from the application menu and confirm the GUI loads.

---

### Step 2 — Download the ISOs

**pfSense:**

Go to pfsense.org/download.

Choose AMD64, DVD Image (ISO) Installer, and a mirror near you.

The file downloads as `.iso.gz`. Decompress:

```
cd ~/Downloads
gunzip pfSense-CE-*.iso.gz
```

**Ubuntu Server 22.04:**

Go to ubuntu.com/download/server. Download the 22.04 LTS ISO.

Verify both:

```
ls -lh ~/Downloads/*.iso
```

---

### Step 3 — Create the pfSense VM

Open VirtualBox. Click New.

Name: pfSense
Type: BSD
Version: FreeBSD (64-bit)

Memory: 1024 MB.

Hard disk: Create a virtual hard disk now. VDI. Dynamically allocated. 20 GB.

Click Create.

Select the pfSense VM. Click Settings.

System: Boot Order — Optical first, then Hard Disk.

Network:
- Adapter 1: enable, Attached to NAT.
- Adapter 2: enable, Attached to Internal Network, Name: labnet.

Storage: under Controller IDE, click the empty CD icon, then the disk icon on the right, choose "Choose a disk file," select the pfSense ISO.

Click OK.

---

### Step 4 — Install pfSense

Start the pfSense VM.

Accept the default boot option.

Follow the installer:
- Accept license.
- Install.
- Choose UFS.
- Confirm disk.
- Wait.
- Reboot.

After reboot, the interface assignment wizard runs:

- VLANs needed? Type n.
- WAN interface: type em0.
- LAN interface: type em1.

The console shows LAN is 192.168.1.1.

Open a browser (from Kali, using the pfSense console link if needed) to https://192.168.1.1. Accept the certificate warning.

Log in: admin / pfsense.

Run the setup wizard:
- Hostname: pfsense
- Domain: lab.local
- Set a new admin password. Save it.
- Timezone: your timezone.
- WAN: accept defaults (it pulls from NAT).
- LAN: accept defaults.
- Reload.

The pfSense dashboard loads with both WAN and LAN up.

Donot add firewall rules yet.


### Step 5 — Create the Wazuh VM

Open VirtualBox. Click New.

Name: Wazuh
Type: Linux
Version: Ubuntu (64-bit)

Memory: 4096 MB.

Hard disk: VDI, dynamically allocated, 50 GB.

Click Create.

Select the Wazuh VM. Click Settings.

System: Boot Order — Optical first.

Network: Adapter 1, Internal Network, labnet.

Storage: mount the Ubuntu Server ISO.

Click OK.

---

### Step 6 — Install Ubuntu Server for Wazuh

Start the Wazuh VM.

Follow the installer:
- Language: English.
- Install Ubuntu Server.
- Keyboard layout.
- Network: accept DHCP. Note the IP it gets.
- Proxy: leave empty.
- Mirror: accept default.
- Storage: entire disk, default layout. Confirm.
- Username: wazuh
- Hostname: wazuh
- Password: strong. Save it.
- Skip Ubuntu Pro.
- Do not install snaps.
- Wait for install.
- Reboot.

Log in.

Set a static IP of 192.168.1.10:

```
sudo nano /etc/netplan/00-installer-config.yaml
```

Replace contents with:

```
network:
  version: 2
  ethernets:
    enp0s3:
      dhcp4: no
      addresses:
        - 192.168.1.10/24
      routes:
        - to: default
          via: 192.168.1.1
      nameservers:
        addresses: [192.168.1.1]
```

Apply:

```
sudo netplan apply
```

Verify:

```
ip a
```

The VM should now have 192.168.1.10.

Test connectivity:

```
ping -c 3 192.168.1.1
```

You should see replies from pfSense.

**Do not install Wazuh yet.**

---

### Step 7 — Create the Target VM

Open VirtualBox. Click New.

Name: Target
Type: Linux
Version: Ubuntu (64-bit)

Memory: 1024 MB.

Hard disk: VDI, dynamically allocated, 10 GB.

Click Create.

Select the Target VM. Click Settings.

System: Boot Order — Optical first.

Network: Adapter 1, Internal Network, labnet.

Storage: mount the Ubuntu Server ISO.

Click OK.

---

### Step 8 — Install Ubuntu Server for the Target

Start the Target VM.

Follow the same installer steps as Step 6, with these differences:
- Username: target
- Hostname: target
- Password: different from Wazuh. Save it.

After reboot, log in.

Set a static IP of 192.168.1.20 using the same netplan method, replacing the address with 192.168.1.20.

Apply:

```
sudo netplan apply
```

Verify:

```
ip a
```

Test connectivity to pfSense:

```
ping -c 3 192.168.1.1
```

Test connectivity to Wazuh:

```
ping -c 3 192.168.1.10
```

Both should succeed.

**Do not install the Wazuh agent yet.**

---

### Step 9 — Add a Host-Only Adapter to pfSense for Kali

In VirtualBox, select the pfSense VM. Click Settings.

Network: enable Adapter 3.

Attached to: Host-only Adapter.

Name: vboxnet0.

Click OK.

Start pfSense.

From the pfSense console menu, choose option 1 to assign interfaces.

Add OPT1 to the new interface (likely em2).

Assign it.

From the pfSense console, choose option 2 to set interface IP addresses.

Set OPT1 to 192.168.56.1/24.

Enable DHCP on OPT1 with range 192.168.56.100 to 192.168.56.200.

On Kali, check vboxnet0:

```
ip a
```

You should see 192.168.56.1 on vboxnet0.

Kali can now reach the lab network through pfSense.

---

### Step 10 — Verify the Full Infrastructure

From Kali:

```
ping -c 3 192.168.56.1
```

This is pfSense's OPT1 interface.

From Kali, reach the Wazuh VM through pfSense:

```
ping -c 3 192.168.1.10
```

If it does not respond, the issue is routing or pfSense firewall rules on OPT1. For this phase, temporarily allow all traffic on OPT1 to confirm the infrastructure works. You will tighten the rules in the next phase.

From Kali, reach the Target:

```
ping -c 3 192.168.1.20
```

From the Wazuh VM:

```
ping -c 3 192.168.1.20
ping -c 3 192.168.1.1
```

From the Target VM:

```
ping -c 3 192.168.1.10
ping -c 3 192.168.1.1
```

All pings should succeed. If they do, the infrastructure is complete.

